During the migration process, the Nautilus DevOps team created several EC2 instances in different regions. 
They are currently in the process of identifying the correct resources and utilization and are making continuous changes to ensure optimal resource utilization. 
Recently, they discovered that one of the EC2 instances was underutilized, prompting them to decide to change the instance type. 
Please make sure the Status check is completed (if it's still in Initializing state) before making any changes to the instance.

Change the instance type from t2.micro to t2.nano for xfusion-ec2 instance using terraform.

Make sure the EC2 instance xfusion-ec2 is in running state after the change.

The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform. Update the main.tf file (do not create a separate .tf file) to change the instance type.

Note: Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
To change the instance type of xfusion-ec2 from t2.micro to t2.nano, you need to modify the instance_type attribute in your existing main.tf file. Since you are using LocalStack (based on your provider.tf), the Terraform apply process will automatically handle the stop/start cycle required for this change.

Updated main.tf
Find the aws_instance resource for xfusion-ec2 in your main.tf and update the instance_type line. The configuration should look similar to this (keep your existing AMI, subnet, and security group values):

hcl
resource "aws_instance" "ec2" {
  ami           = "ami-0c101f26f147fa7fd" # Keep your existing AMI
  instance_type = "t2.nano"               # Changed from t2.micro
  subnet_id     = ""                      # Keep your existing value
  vpc_security_group_ids = [
    "sg-1a0391390d2b1259b"                # Keep your existing value
  ]

  tags = {
    Name = "xfusion-ec2"
  }
}
Verification Before Applying
The task explicitly asks you to ensure the status check is completed before making changes. You can verify the current instance status using the AWS CLI against your LocalStack endpoint :

bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-instance-status --instance-ids <your-instance-id>
Wait until the InstanceStatus and SystemStatus show as ok (not initializing).

Execution Steps
Navigate and Init: cd /home/bob/terraform and run terraform init (if not already done).

Plan: Run terraform plan. You should see an in-place update showing instance_type = "t2.micro" -> "t2.nano" .

Apply: Run terraform apply -auto-approve. Terraform will stop the instance, modify the type, and start it again.

Verify State: After apply completes, confirm the instance is running with the new type:

bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids <your-instance-id> --query 'Reservations[0].Instances[0].{State:State.Name, Type:InstanceType}'
The output should show "State": "running" and "Type": "t2.nano".

Final Check: Run terraform plan again. It should return "No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration."

